  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=6?$",
  "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,3)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$",
